<title>Hemoptysis (Nonmassive): Management.</title>
<graphic href="eee0674-w001.gif" />
<cell type="start" id="cell-1">
<label>History and physical examination, pH, and appearance of material suggest hemoptysis and lower respiratory tract source?</label>
<path target="cell-2">No</path>
<path target="cell-3">Yes</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-2">
<label>Hematemesis or upper airway source. Treat appropriately, consider referral for upper endoscopy.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-3">
<label>Order CT or CTA of chest with IV contrast or CXR. </label>
<path target="cell-4">Normal</path>
<path target="cell-5">Abnormal</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-4">
<label>History suggests RTI.</label>
<path target="cell-6">No</path>
<path target="cell-7">Yes</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-6">
<label>Observe, or consider bronchoscopy or high-resolution chest CT.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-7">
<label>Consider antibiotics. Symptoms resolve?</label>
<path target="cell-8">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-5">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-8">
<label>No further evaluation.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-5">
<label>High resolution chest CT &#x00B1; bronchoscopy.</label>
<path target="cell-9">Specific diagnosis</path>
<path target="cell-10">Nondiagnostic</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-9">
<label>Treat as appropriate.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-10">
<label>Laboratory evaluation: Initially INR, CBC with platelets, sedimentation rate, sputum for gram stain, acid fast bacilli, cytology, and PPD test.</label>
<path target="cell-9"></path>
</cell>
</algorithm>
